{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underpricing: when the last 10–15 days show a persistent rise (higher highs / higher lows), add a positive level shift to the whole path. Heuristic: +2 to +4 EUR for days 1–7, rising to +8 to +12 EUR by days 15–30.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias increases with lead time: near-term (1–5d) errors are small; medium/long horizon (10–30d) forecasts are systematically too low. Apply an error ramp with horizon (e.g., +0.3 to +0.5 EUR per forecast day after day 5, capped around +10 to +15 EUR).",
    "Trend lag: forecasts follow the direction but react too slowly to regime shifts upward. If 5-day return > +3% and 10-day MA slope is positive, increase the forecast slope (steepen the trajectory) rather than only shifting levels.",
    "False mean reversion: model repeatedly pulls prices back toward ~68–71 even when realized prices keep trending to mid/high 70s and 80s. When realized breaks above recent 30d range/top quartile, suppress mean-reversion terms (or widen the equilibrium band upward).",
    "Post-spike behavior: the model expects pullbacks after sharp rises, but realized series often consolidates at higher levels instead of reverting. After a >+2 EUR day (or >+4 EUR over 3 days), reduce any downward correction in the next 5–10 forecast days and keep levels elevated (e.g., maintain at least 50–70% of the spike gain)."
  ]
}
